k colony near Naples.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/cumana"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/cumana.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Cumaná</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Cumaná is a city on the north-eastern coast of Venezuela, capital of Sucre state.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/cumans"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/cumans.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Cumans</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Cumans were a Turkic nomadic people of the Eurasian steppe, closely linked with the Kipchaks and called Polovtsians in Russian sources.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/cumberland"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/cumberland.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Cumberland</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Cumberland is a historic county in north-west England, between the Solway Firth and the Lake District, with Carlisle as its chief town.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/cumbria"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/cumbria.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Cumbria</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Cumbria is a county of north-west England, bordering Scotland, created in 1974 mainly from Cumberland and Westmorland.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/cumbric"><span class="enc-name">Cumbric</span><span class="enc-type">Term</span><span class="enc-snip">Cumbric was the Brittonic Celtic language of the early medieval north of Britain, spoken in the kingdom of Strathclyde and in Cumbria.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/cunedda"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/cunedda.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Cunedda</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Cunedda is a figure of Welsh tradition said to have come with his sons from Manaw Gododdin, near the head of the Firth of Forth, in the 5th century to drive the Irish…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/cuneglasus"><span class="enc-name">Cuneglasus</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Cuneglasus was a 6th-century British king denounced by Gildas in his De Excidio Britanniae, who called him the charioteer of the Bear's Stronghold and accused him of…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/cuneiform"><span class="enc-name">Cuneiform</span><span class="enc-type">Term</span><span class="enc-snip">Cuneiform is a writing system of wedge-shaped signs pressed into clay tablets, invented in Mesopotamia in the late fourth millennium BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/cuneyd-bey"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/cuneyd-bey.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Cüneyd Bey</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Cüneyd Bey was a Turkish lord who held Smyrna and fought to restore the Aydinid beylik in the decades after Timur's victory over the Ottomans in 1402.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/cunigunde-of-luxembourg"><span class="enc-name">Cunigunde of Luxembourg</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Cunigunde of Luxembourg was the wife of Emperor Henry II and was crowned empress in 1014.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/cunimund"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/cunimund.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Cunimund</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Cunimund was the last king of the Gepids, ruling from about 560 from the region of Sirmium.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/cunipert"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/cunipert.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Cunipert</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Cunipert was King of the Lombards, co-ruler with his father Perctarit from 680 and sole king from 688 to 700.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/cunobelinus"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/cunobelinus.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Cunobelinus</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Cunobelinus was a king of the Catuvellauni who ruled much of south-eastern Britain from about 9 to 40 CE from his capital at Camulodunum, modern Colchester.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/cuntisuyu"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/cuntisuyu.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Cuntisuyu</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Cuntisuyu was the south-western quarter of the Inca Empire, reaching from Cusco to the Pacific coast of southern Peru.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/cupbearer"><span class="enc-name">Cupbearer</span><span class="enc-type">Term</span><span class="enc-snip">A cupbearer was a court official who served drinks to the king, a position of trust and access to the ruler.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/cupids"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/cupids.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Cupids</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Cupids, originally Cuper's Cove, is a town on Conception Bay in Newfoundland, Canada.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/cupisnique"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/cupisnique.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Cupisnique</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">Cupisnique was a culture of the north coast of Peru, flourishing from about 1500 to 500 BCE in valleys such as Jequetepeque, Chicama and Moche.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/cura-annonae"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/cura-annonae.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Cura Annonae</span><span class="enc-type">Institution</span><span class="enc-snip">The cura annonae was the Roman system for supplying grain to the city of Rome, including the free distribution of grain, the annona, to citizens.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/cures"><span class="enc-name">Cures</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Cures was an ancient town of the Sabines north-east of Rome, near modern Fara in Sabina.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/curicaueri"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/curicaueri.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Curicaueri</span><span class="enc-type">Religion / idea</span><span class="enc-snip">Curicaueri was the fire and sun god of the Purépecha of western Mexico, patron of the ruling Uacúsecha lineage and of the Purépecha state.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/curse-of-agade"><span class="enc-name">Curse of Agade</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Curse of Agade is a Sumerian literary composition, probably of the late 3rd millennium BCE, explaining the fall of the city of Akkad.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/cursus-publicus"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/cursus-publicus.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Cursus publicus</span><span class="enc-type">Institution</span><span class="enc-snip">The cursus publicus was the state courier and transport service of the Roman Empire, established by Augustus, with relay stations along the main roads.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/curt-von-francois"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/curt-von-francois.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Curt von François</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Curt von François was a German army officer and explorer in Africa.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/curtis-island"><span class="enc-name">Curtis Island</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Curtis Island is a large island off the coast of central Queensland, Australia, near the port of Gladstone.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/cusco"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/cusco.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Cusco</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Cusco is a city in the Andes of southern Peru, about 3,400 metres above sea level, which was the capital of the Inca Empire.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/cusco-school"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/cusco-school.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Cusco School</span><span class="enc-type">Religion / idea</span><span class="enc-snip">The Cusco School was a tradition of Catholic religious painting centred on Cusco in the Viceroyalty of Peru from the late sixteenth to the eighteenth century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/custom-of-paris"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/custom-of-paris.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Custom of Paris</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Custom of Paris was the body of customary law of the region of Paris, first written down in 1510 and revised in 1580.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/customs-and-commercial-revenue"><span class="enc-name">Customs and commercial revenue</span><span class="enc-type">Historical factor</span><span class="enc-snip">Customs and commercial revenue refers to state income from duties, tolls and taxes on trade, as opposed to land taxes.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/customs-union"><span class="enc-name">Customs union</span><span class="enc-type">Term</span><span class="enc-snip">A customs union is an agreement among territories to remove tariffs between them and adopt a common external tariff.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/cut-nyak-dhien"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/cut-nyak-dhien.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Cut Nyak Dhien</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Cut Nyak Dhien was an Acehnese guerrilla leader in the Aceh War against the Dutch.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/cuthred-of-wessex"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/cuthred-of-wessex.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Cuthred of Wessex</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Cuthred was king of Wessex from 740 to 756.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/cuttack"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/cuttack.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Cuttack</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Cuttack is a city in Odisha, India, at the head of the Mahanad