ss="enc-snip">Cynllaith was a medieval commote in north-east Wales, in the valley of the River Cynllaith on the borders of modern Powys and Wrexham.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/cynric"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/cynric.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Cynric</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Cynric is named by the Anglo-Saxon Chronicle as the son, or in some sources the grandson, of Cerdic and his successor as king of Wessex, ruling from 534 to 560.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/cyprian"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/cyprian.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Cyprian</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Cyprian was bishop of Carthage from about 249 and one of the most influential Latin Christian writers.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/cypro-minoan-syllabary"><span class="enc-name">Cypro-Minoan syllabary</span><span class="enc-type">Term</span><span class="enc-snip">The Cypro-Minoan syllabary is an undeciphered script used on Cyprus in the Late Bronze Age, from about 1550 to 1050 BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/cyprus"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/cyprus.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Cyprus</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Cyprus is a large island in the eastern Mediterranean, valued for its copper.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/cyprus-convention"><span class="enc-name">Cyprus Convention</span><span class="enc-type">Treaty · 1878</span><span class="enc-snip">The Cyprus Convention was a secret agreement signed on 4 June 1878 between Britain and the Ottoman Empire.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/cypselus"